#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f10a926-3cfd-5575-ae19-59f95cdca45f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Real Root"}] :question/id #uuid "a6d40e97-c5d4-55e1-8a4c-56be1fc2d731" :question/math-academy-id "e-5794" :question/problem "Given that $x=-2$ is a solution to the equation $x^3+8 = 0$, find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3+8\n$$\n\nSince $x=-2$ is a root of $f(x)$, then according to the factor theorem $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$.\n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}8; - 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x+2)(x^2-2x+4)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2-2x+4 = 0\n$$\n\nby completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= -3 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= (\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\nx - 1 &= ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= 1 ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are\n\n$$\nx=-2, \\, 1\\pm \\sqrt3\\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175734 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/id #uuid "054cbbc8-22ef-585d-bf8a-849eabdc09a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8800" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to $x^3-3x^2+12x-10 = 0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx_1=1+3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nis a root of $f(x)$, the complex conjugate\n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x)$.\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem,\n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x)$. Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - (1 + 3\\textrm{i}))(x - (1 - 3\\textrm{i})) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - (1 + 3\\textrm{i} + 1 - 3\\textrm{i})x + (1 + 3\\textrm{i})(1 - 3\\textrm{i}) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + (1^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2x + 10 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 2x + 10x - 1; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 12x - 10; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 2x^{2} + 10x_(―); x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 0x^{2} + 02x - 10; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3}- 0x^{2} + 02x - 10_(―); x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 12x - 10\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\nx - 1 = 0⟹x = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are\n\n$$\nx=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d4640a-7f5c-5644-aa43-88283bdb1419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173251} {:answer/id #uuid "95b59b75-0b50-5ba5-acf1-dd4bb3d10abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{8}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173252} {:answer/id #uuid "a252ec0f-a961-57b8-b0ee-8e902babccce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{2}, 2 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173253} {:answer/id #uuid "b431f70b-c50f-5aaf-8306-53623badba1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.ty